NNAAMMEE
     sspplliitt — split a file into pieces

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     sspplliitt [--aa _s_u_f_f_i_x___l_e_n_g_t_h]
           [--bb _b_y_t_e___c_o_u_n_t[kk|mm] | --ll _l_i_n_e___c_o_u_n_t | --pp _p_a_t_t_e_r_n] [_f_i_l_e [_n_a_m_e]]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The sspplliitt utility reads the given _f_i_l_e, or standard input if no file is
     specified, and breaks it up into files of 1000 lines each.  _f_i_l_e itself
     is not altered.

     The options are as follows:

     --aa _s_u_f_f_i_x___l_e_n_g_t_h
             Use _s_u_f_f_i_x___l_e_n_g_t_h letters to form the suffix of the file name
             (see below).  The default suffix length is 2.

     --bb _b_y_t_e___c_o_u_n_t[kk|mm]
             Create files _b_y_t_e___c_o_u_n_t bytes in length.  If ‘k’ is appended to
             the number, the file is split into _b_y_t_e___c_o_u_n_t kilobyte pieces.
             If ‘m’ is appended to the number, the file is split into
             _b_y_t_e___c_o_u_n_t megabyte pieces.

     --ll _l_i_n_e___c_o_u_n_t
             Create files _l_i_n_e___c_o_u_n_t lines in length.

     --pp _p_a_t_t_e_r_n
             The file is split whenever an input line matches _p_a_t_t_e_r_n, which
             is interpreted as an extended regular expression.  The matching
             line will be the first line of the next output file.  This option
             is incompatible with the --bb and --ll options.

     If _n_a_m_e is specified, it is used as a prefix for the names of the files
     into which the file is split.  In this case, each file into which the
     file is split is named by the prefix followed by a lexically ordered suf‐
     fix using _s_u_f_f_i_x___l_e_n_g_t_h characters in the range “a-z”.

     If the _n_a_m_e argument is not specified, the file is split into lexically
     ordered files named with the prefixes ‘x’, ‘y’, and ‘z’.

EEXXIITT SSTTAATTUUSS
     The sspplliitt utility exits 0 on success, and >0 if an error occurs.

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     csplit(1), re_format(7)

SSTTAANNDDAARRDDSS
     The sspplliitt utility is compliant with the IEEE Std 1003.1-2008 (“POSIX.1”)
     specification.

     The flag [--pp], as well as the ‘y’ and ‘z’ prefixes in the absence of a
     _n_a_m_e parameter, are an extension to that specification.

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     A sspplliitt command appeared in Version 3 AT&T UNIX.

BBUUGGSS
     The maximum line length for matching patterns is 65536.

